Git & GitHub – Hands-on Cheat Sheet
1. Install Git and Configure Username & Email
Aim: Install Git and configure user details.

harisimple16-crypto



Commands:
git --version

git config --global user.name "Your Name"

git config --global user.email "you@example.com"

git config --global --list


Result: Git installed and user configuration completed.

2. Create Local Repository and Perform Three Commits
Aim: Create a local repository and perform three commits.

Procedure:
1. Create website folder.
2. Create index.html and style.css.
3. Add HTML and CSS code.
4. Initialize Git.
5. Stage and commit files separately.
6. Modify index.html and perform third commit.
Commands:
mkdir website
cd website

git init
git status

git add index.html
git commit -m "Add index.html"

git add style.css
git commit -m "Add style.css"

git add index.html
git commit -m "Add new paragraph"

git log --oneline


Result: Local repository created with three commits.

3. Create GitHub Repository and Push/Pull
Aim: Connect a local repository to GitHub and perform push and pull operations.

Procedure:
1. Create a new repository on GitHub named website.
2. Keep README, .gitignore and License unchecked.
3. Connect the local repository.
4. Push files to GitHub.
5. Make a change on GitHub and pull it locally.
Commands:
git remote add origin <repository-url>

git remote -v

git branch -M master

git push -u origin master

git pull origin master


For subsequent changes:
git add .
git commit -m "message"
git push origin master


Result: Local repository successfully connected and synchronized with GitHub.

4. Git Branching and Merging
Aim: Create a branch, make changes, merge it into master and delete the branch.

Procedure:
1. Create myproject folder and initialize Git.
2. Create file1.txt on master and commit.
3. Create and switch to feature-branch.
4. Modify the file and commit.
5. Switch back to master.
6. Merge the feature branch.
7. Verify and delete the branch.
Commands:
mkdir myproject
cd myproject

git init

git add file1.txt
git commit -m "This file is from Master"

git branch feature-branch

git switch feature-branch

git add file1.txt
git commit -m "Add new code in feature-branch"

git switch master

git merge feature-branch

cat file1.txt

git log --oneline

git branch -d feature-branch
